What is not rgx's code
----------------------
No source file of any upstream project is included in this repository. What
the tree carries of other people is *knowledge* — the layout of a file
format — and the record of where that knowledge came from. The code that
encodes it was written here, in this tree's idiom, and it is held against
the corpora each format's own producer writes.

crates/rgx-adapters-inspect/src/cad_sat.rs
    The ACIS SAT record layout, learned from the writer side of ezdxf
    (https://github.com/mozman/ezdxf).

crates/rgx-adapters-archive/src/borg.rs
    The borg segment framing, the payload envelope, the compressor tags and
    the item metadata, learned from borg 1.2's own source
    (https://github.com/borgbackup/borg).

crates/rgx-adapters-archive/src/pgdump.rs
    The custom-dump framing and the table of contents, learned from
    PostgreSQL's own `pg_dump` source (https://github.com/postgres/postgres).

crates/rgx-adapters-tabular/src/databases.rs
    The Redis RDB type table and length encoding, learned from Redis's own
    source (https://github.com/redis/redis), and the LZF decompressor,
    written from liblzf's `lzfP.h` (https://github.com/libarchive/liblzf),
    which Redis vendors rather than depends on.

crates/rgx-adapters-tabular/src/lmdb.rs
    The `MDB_meta` layout, learned from LMDB's own `mdb.c`
    (https://github.com/LMDB/lmdb).

crates/rgx-core/src/memmap2/
    An independent reimplementation of `memmap2-rs` upstream commit f16835d
    (v0.9.0), Copyright 2015 Dan Burkert and 2020 Yevhenii Reizner, whose
    public API was read to learn the contract. No upstream source file is in
    the tree. Upstream offered its work under MIT OR Apache-2.0; the
    Apache-2.0 option is the one taken here.

crates/rgx-core/src/frequency.rs
    The byte-rank table in `RANK` is copied from the `memchr` crate
    (https://github.com/BurntSushi/memchr), which offers MIT OR Apache-2.0;
    the Apache-2.0 option is the one taken. The file's own header says that
    the values are copied, not measured here.

crates/rgx-adapters-office/src/visio.rs, ppt.rs, tex.rs, xls.rs,
crates/rgx-adapters-media/src/avi.rs, flv.rs, mpeg.rs, mp4mkv.rs,
crates/rgx-adapters-inspect/src/x509.rs, crates/rgx-adapters-docs/src/lib.rs
    Reduced ports of readers of the `io` project by Dan Gohman
    (dev@sunfishcode.online), which offers MIT OR Apache-2.0; the Apache-2.0
    option is the one taken. The Visio reader's grammar was read from
    LibreOffice's `libvisio`, the reconstruction of the format that exists;
    no libvisio code is in this tree.

crates/rgx-adapters-inspect/testdata/sample.plt
    A capture from an HP 8595E plotter, taken from the HPGL-Plotter project
    (https://github.com/VK2BEA/HPGL-Plotter), used unmodified.
    Apache License 2.0. Copyright 2022 Michael G. Katzmann.

The corpora under `crates/*/testdata/` are real files, taken from the
reference implementations of the formats they exercise rather than written
by hand, and each is covered by its own project's licence — BSD-3-Clause for
most of them, Apache-2.0 for the HPGL-Plotter capture, the terms of their own
projects for the rest. The README of each fixture directory says which file
came from which project and what its terms are. They are data, not code, and
none of them carries a licence obligation on this code.

Third-party components in a distributed binary
----------------------------------------------
A distributed `rgx` binary is not only rgx's code: it links the Rust
dependencies, and each of them carries its own permissive license (MIT,
MIT OR Apache-2.0, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, Zlib, CC0-1.0, Unicode-3.0,
0BSD, ISC, and similar). Those are notices, not obligations on this code.
The full set can be reproduced with:

    cargo metadata --format-version 1 --locked \
      | jq -r '.packages[].license' | sort -u

Lineage
-------
rgx was inspired by ripgrep-all (https://github.com/phiresky/ripgrep-all)
and is CLI-compatible with ripgrep where it extends it. No ripgrep-all
source files are included in this project: every file in this repository
was written for rgx. Behavioral parity, flag semantics, and adapter
concepts are ideas, not code, and are not subject to copyright.

Two lineages are worth naming, because a reader who wants to know what the
code is standing on deserves to be told:

  * The 7z tree (`crates/rgx-adapters-archive/src/sevenz/`) was written from
    the LZMA SDK (https://7-zip.org/sdk.html), which its publisher placed in
    the public domain: "Anyone is free to copy, modify, publish, use,
    compile, sell, or distribute the original LZMA SDK code, either in
    source code form or as a compiled binary, for any purpose, commercial
    or non-commercial, and by any means." (Copyright (C) Igor Pavlov.)
    Public domain carries no conditions, so that tree is licensed here
    under Apache-2.0 like the rest.

